# Settings for the live tomscoding.com deployment, minus the secrets.
#
# Everything here is already public — the domains resolve in DNS, and the
# contact address goes to Let's Encrypt. The two passwords are deliberately
# absent, so this file is safe to keep in git; append them on the server:
#
#   cp env.tomscoding .env
#   echo "TOMSCODING_PASSWORD=your-passphrase" >> .env
#   echo "TOMSCODING_PASSWORD_2=her-passphrase" >> .env
#
# .env is gitignored, so the passwords never reach the repository.

TOMSCODING_DOMAIN=code.tomscoding.com
TOMSCODING_LANDING_DOMAIN=tomscoding.com
TOMSCODING_ACME_EMAIL=tommyshanahan@gmail.com

TZ=Asia/Shanghai

# Sized for the 4 GB Vultr instance: 2g + 1g of container ceilings leaves
# about a gigabyte for the host and Caddy. The repo defaults assume 8 GB and
# would let a build take the machine down.
TOMSCODING_WORKSPACE_MEMORY=2g
TOMSCODING_CPU_SHARES=1024

# Second seat.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=seat2
TOMSCODING_DOMAIN_2=her.tomscoding.com
TOMSCODING_WORKSPACE_2_MEMORY=1g
TOMSCODING_CPU_SHARES_2=512

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser on the VPS. Pages load from Tokyo and the screen is streamed to you,
# so the machine's reach becomes yours — no second device to fetch things on.
#
# Off by default: add "browser" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above (comma-separated,
# e.g. COMPOSE_PROFILES=seat2,browser) and give it a password below. Needs its
# own A record, same as the other hostnames.
#
# On a 4 GB box this over-commits: 2g + 1g + 1g of ceilings plus the host.
# Ceilings are not reservations, and there is swap, so light use is fine — but
# if it starts feeling slow, that is the box telling you to resize to 8 GB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOMSCODING_BROWSER_DOMAIN=browser.tomscoding.com
TOMSCODING_BROWSER_USER=tom
TOMSCODING_BROWSER_MEMORY=1g
TOMSCODING_BROWSER_CPU_SHARES=512

# Shared memory for tab contents. This comes out of the ceiling above rather
# than being extra, so keeping it well under that leaves Firefox room to run in
# what is left. Set it equal to the ceiling and heavy tabs can consume the whole
# allowance, the kernel kills Firefox, and the screen goes black with the
# container still up. A watchdog restarts it when that happens; a bigger box is
# what stops it happening.
TOMSCODING_BROWSER_SHM=512m

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The agent chat app — our own interface, in front of the Claude Agent SDK.
#
# Off by default: add "agent" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above and give it both a
# password and an ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. It needs its own A record.
#
# The API key is what makes this work where the browser login does not: the
# request to Anthropic is made by this server, from Tokyo, and the browser
# never contacts Anthropic at all.
#
# Leave the model unset unless a run reports an unknown model — the SDK picks
# a sensible default, and a wrong string here breaks every turn.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOMSCODING_AGENT_DOMAIN=agent.tomscoding.com
TOMSCODING_AGENT_MEMORY=1g
TOMSCODING_AGENT_CPU_SHARES=512
# TOMSCODING_AGENT_MODEL=

# The word the owner's seat asks for when it hits a decision that is yours
# rather than its own — publishing to live readers, deleting work, acting on
# data about an identifiable person. It says what the decision is, you give the
# word, it proceeds.
#
# It is not a permission and nothing is gated on it. That seat could already do
# all of it; this only stops a judgement call turning into a negotiation. It is
# not passed to the partner seats, and saying it to one would achieve nothing
# anyway — what a seat can do is its mounts and its tool deny list, and no
# conversation reaches either.
#
# Set it in .env, not here: this file is committed and that one is not.
# Unset, there is no word and the seat behaves as it always did.
# Where each project can be seen running, as name=url pairs. Clicking a project
# in Sage's Projects panel opens that project's own app, instead of the one
# address a seat used to have — which could only ever be right for one project.
#
#   TOMSCODING_PROJECT_APPS="study-pal=https://liuxuesheng.help"
#
# A project with no entry says so rather than showing another project's app.
# This is a map and not a guess on purpose: a dev server started inside the
# agent container has no host port and cannot be reached from a browser, so an
# inferred address would be a plausible wrong URL rather than a preview.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Video generation — Seedance, on BytePlus
#
# BytePlus is ByteDance's international arm. The mainland console (Volcengine)
# runs the same models but wants a Chinese entity and real-name verification;
# BytePlus takes a foreign company and an ordinary card, which is the route that
# works from here.
#
# The key is the only thing that has to be set. The base URL and the model are
# here because the API shape was assembled from secondary sources — the official
# reference was unreachable when this was written — so if a call fails with a
# shape complaint, these are the two lines to change rather than the code.
#
# Use the ENDPOINT id, not the model name. This is the thing that costs an hour
# if you get it wrong, because both fail and neither error says what is
# actually wrong:
#
#   the model name with its version  -> 404 "has not activated the model"
#   the model name without a version -> 404 "does not exist"
#
# Both of those appear even when the console plainly says Activated. Activating
# a model creates a preset inference endpoint, and that is what the API answers
# to. Find it at Console -> Inference -> Online inference: the row shows the
# model id and, beneath it, an id starting `ep-m-`. Use that one.
#
#   TOMSCODING_ARK_VIDEO_MODEL=ep-m-20260904014924-dd8l2
#
# Activate models under Console -> Model activation -> Media. The Activate
# control is in the last column of that table, off-screen at normal zoom.
#
# TOMSCODING_ARK_API_KEY=
# TOMSCODING_ARK_BASE=https://ark.ap-southeast.bytepluses.com
# TOMSCODING_ARK_VIDEO_MODEL=seedance-1-0-lite-t2v-250428

# Clips per day, per seat. This is the one feature that costs real money on
# every press; a retry loop with no ceiling is an invoice rather than a message.
TOMSCODING_VIDEO_DAILY_LIMIT=20

# Let the partner seat generate video too. Off by default: the reason here is
# money rather than secrecy — that seat would be spending on your account. The
# key is passed only while this is set, so with it off that container cannot
# generate anything whatever its code says. Its own smaller daily ceiling.
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER_VIDEO=1
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER_VIDEO_LIMIT=6

# TOMSCODING_PROJECT_APPS=

# TOMSCODING_AGENT_CLEARANCE_WORD=

# A word the PARTNER seat must type before anything reaches readers —
# publishing a series, or deleting one. Enforced by the route, so the page
# cannot skip it and the agent on that seat cannot be talked out of it. Drafts
# and cover uploads are not gated: asking twelve times while somebody writes
# twelve beats teaches them to keep it in the clipboard.
#
# Be clear about what it buys. It stops an unconsidered or unilateral publish,
# which is the real worry with a live catalogue and someone else's hands. It is
# not the owner approving each publish — it is a shared secret, so once the
# partner knows it they keep it. Approval would mean their publish landing in a
# queue you release.
#
# Set it in .env, not here. Use a different string from the owner's clearance
# word: the partner has to be told this one.
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER_PUBLISH_WORD=

# Show the partner seat the numbers — the readout in its masthead and the full
# dashboard behind it. Granted the same way as the story desk: set it to 1, and
# delete the line to take it back. With it off, the counter's address and shared
# token are not passed to that container at all, so the seat cannot reach the
# counter even if the check were edited away.
#
# Be clear what it hands over, because "analytics" sounds smaller than it is:
# not just today's count, but where people are, how they arrived, which share
# link sent them, day by day, for both products. For a marketing partner that is
# the job. For anyone else it is the business's state of health.
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER_NUMBERS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A seat for a business partner.
#
# Same app as the agent, configured so that it can read one project and write
# mockups, and nothing else. The limits are structural, not instructions: the
# source is mounted read-only, the only writable path is the mockups folder,
# and that container has its own home with no git credentials in it.
#
# Off by default: add "partner" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above, give it a password in
# .env, point it at a branch, and run `make partner-sync` before `make up`.
# Needs its own A record.
#
# The snapshot is the point. It changes when you run `make partner-sync` and at
# no other time — nothing they do can advance it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_DOMAIN=partner.tomscoding.com
# His sign-in name. The password goes in .env beside the others, never here.
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_USER=Brendan
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_PROJECT=Liuxuesheng
# The repositories Brendan is allowed to see, and the exact branch of each.
#
# Study Pal is its own repository now — it used to be the /talk route inside
# Fern (journey.git#aesthetic-spike) and is not there any more. Pointing this
# at the old branch would show him a version of the product that no longer
# exists.
# One entry per line as url#branch. This list IS the access decision: anything
# not on it is not on that machine, so there is nothing to reach or guess at.
#
# Change it, run `make partner-sync`, and that becomes what is on his screen.
# Until then nothing moves.
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_REPOS="https://github.com/tommyshanahan-max/study-pal.git#main"
# Documents Brendan can open, under Documents in the masthead. One per line,
# path relative to the snapshot, optionally `path=Title`. Paths start with the
# folder name the repo was synced into.
#
# A list, deliberately — not "every .md in the snapshot". A repository's docs
# folder holds working notes as readily as it holds anything meant for someone
# else, and under a scan the first thing a new file does is publish itself.
#
# The repository has three at the top level. Exactly one of them is here, and
# the reasons for the other two are the point of naming documents one by one:
#
#   README.md    what the product is, what it does, how it deploys. Listed.
#   HANDOFF.md   internal engineering — the move out of the Fern repo, the
#                redirects, a path on your own laptop. Nothing damaging, and
#                nothing he needs to review an app or ask for a mockup. Add it
#                if he starts asking how it is put together.
#   TODO.md      DO NOT LIST. It is about WHOIS privacy, what Vultr discloses
#                under legal process, the card the account is billed to,
#                whether to form a company — and "giving a partner a defined
#                share instead of a handshake". That last line is about him.
#                It is your side of a negotiation with the person this seat is
#                for, and it is exactly what a directory scan would have handed
#                over without anyone deciding to.
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_DOCS="study-pal/README.md=What Study Pal is"

# Files held out of the snapshot entirely. Paths relative to each repository's
# folder, comma or newline separated.
#
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER_REPOS decides which repositories are on that machine; this
# decides which files inside them are. Both are needed, and the second one is
# easy to forget: a repository is rarely written for one audience. Study Pal's
# README is exactly what a partner should read. Its TODO.md discusses what
# share to give that same partner, and docs/PRIVACY.md covers what the host
# discloses under legal process and the owner's own position.
#
# Naming a document in TOMSCODING_PARTNER_DOCS is not the control — that panel
# is a convenience. The agent can read anything on that disk if it is asked to.
# The only reliable way to not show a file is for it not to be there.
#
# docs/PITCH.md is deliberately NOT excluded: it is the argument for the
# product, written to be handed to exactly this reader.
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_EXCLUDE="TODO.md, docs/PRIVACY.md"

# The live app, shown in a frame beside the conversation. Optional — unset, the
# seat is exactly as it was and no preview is offered.
#
# It is a reviewer's seat: without this he describes a screen he cannot see
# while typing about it. Mockups open in the same frame rather than a new tab,
# so the conversation that asked for one stays next to it.
#
# The frame is the app's own site, loaded in the reader's browser — this server
# does not fetch or proxy it. A site that refuses to be framed shows blank, and
# the panel carries an Open link for exactly that case.
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_APP_URL="https://liuxuesheng.help"

TOMSCODING_PARTNER_MEMORY=768m
TOMSCODING_PARTNER_CPU_SHARES=256

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The seat that runs The Feed
#
# Its own hostname, its own login, its own snapshot. Shared by more than one
# person on purpose — so understand what that means before handing the password
# on: there is no way to tell afterwards which of them did a thing, and
# whatever one of them can reach, the other can.
#
# What it can do:
#   - moderate The Feed: release or remove posts, release or refuse profile
#     photographs. That is user-submitted content and it is this seat's job.
#   - read the board's source and build mockups of changes.
#
# What it cannot do, and not because a rule says so:
#   - change the app. The source is mounted read-only and the only writable
#     path is the mockups directory.
#   - commit or deploy. The container has no git identity, no remote, and no
#     path to the host. There is nothing here to commit with and nowhere to
#     deploy to, so no password in that conversation unlocks it. Changes are
#     mocked up there and shipped from the owner's own seat.
#   - reach any other product on this box. It holds BOARD_ADMIN_KEY and no
#     other credential, and it is on a network where nothing else resolves.
#
# Off by default: add "thefeed" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above, give it a password in
# .env, and run `make feed-sync` before `make up`. Needs its own A record.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOMSCODING_FEED_DOMAIN=thefeed.tomscoding.com
# The sign-in name shown in the masthead. The password goes in .env, never here.
TOMSCODING_FEED_USER=Tom
TOMSCODING_FEED_PROJECT="The Feed"
# What the assistant on this seat is called. Two seats are open at once on the
# same screen, and both answering to "Sage" is how somebody tells the wrong one
# to do something — the one that can moderate a live board being the wrong one
# to be unsure about. Only a name: it changes nothing about what the seat can
# reach, which is set by its mounts, its tools and its credentials.
TOMSCODING_FEED_ASSISTANT="The Professor"
# The repository this seat sees, and the exact branch. This list IS the access
# decision: anything not on it is not on that machine, so there is nothing to
# reach or guess at. Change it, run `make feed-sync`, and that becomes what is
# on their screen. Until then nothing moves.
#
# Deliberately only the board. Nothing about any other product is on this seat,
# and that is not tidiness — a Sage that half-remembers a second app gives
# confidently wrong answers about this one.
TOMSCODING_FEED_REPOS="https://github.com/tommyshanahan-max/sage.git#main"
# Documents they can open, under Documents in the masthead. One per line, path
# relative to the snapshot, optionally `path=Title`.
#
# A list, deliberately — not "every .md in the snapshot". A repository's docs
# folder holds working notes as readily as it holds anything meant for someone
# else, and under a scan the first thing a new file does is publish itself.
TOMSCODING_FEED_DOCS="sage/board/README.md=What The Feed is"
# What goes ON that machine, named. An allow list, not a deny list, and the
# difference is the whole point on a seat two people share.
#
# The repository above holds every product on this box: the admin panel, the
# deployment layout, the Caddy config, the settings preset. A deny list would
# have to name each of those and would fail open the day a new one is added —
# the first thing a new file would do is publish itself. This fails closed: a
# path not on this line is not on that machine, so there is nothing to reach,
# guess at, or be talked into.
#
# One entry, on purpose: the board and nothing else.
TOMSCODING_FEED_INCLUDE="board"
# The live app, shown in a frame beside the conversation, and the address a
# released post can be checked at. The reader's own browser loads it; this
# server does not fetch or proxy it.
#
# The feed rather than the landing page. Somebody on this seat is moderating or
# proposing a change to the thing people actually read; the page explaining
# what the product is costs them a tap before they can see any of it, every
# time they open the frame.
TOMSCODING_FEED_APP_URL="https://liuxuesheng.io/feed"
# How the seat reaches the board to moderate it. By service name over the
# private network by default, so the admin key never leaves this box to release
# a post. Only set this if the board moves somewhere else.
# TOMSCODING_FEED_BOARD_BASE=http://board:8080
#
# The numbers, granted the same way as on a partner seat: unset, that seat has
# no address for the counter and no token to ask with, so the panel reports a
# switch that is off. Set to 1 to show it the readout and the dashboard behind
# it — which is where people are, how they arrived and which share link sent
# them, day by day.
# TOMSCODING_FEED_NUMBERS=1
TOMSCODING_FEED_MEMORY=768m
TOMSCODING_FEED_CPU_SHARES=256

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A second partner seat, for someone who should NOT see the first one's work.
#
# Own login, own snapshot, own mockups, own hostname, own network — the two
# cannot reach each other. That separation is the reason to run a second
# container rather than a second account in the first.
#
# Off by default: add "partner2" to COMPOSE_PROFILES, set a user and password
# in .env, list their repos below, and run `make partner-sync-2` before
# `make up`. Needs its own A record.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_DOMAIN=partner.newdomain.com
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_USER=
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_PROJECT=
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_REPOS=""
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_DOCS=""
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_MEMORY=768m
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_CPU_SHARES=256

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The brand's homepage.
#
# A public page — no password, no container, no profile to enable. Caddy serves
# ./brand from disk, so it costs nothing to run and cannot be signed in to.
#
# It carries the brand, a link to the live app, and an Admin list holding the
# seats above. That list is a set of links: each seat still asks for its own
# username and password on its own address, which is the only place a password
# is ever typed. Nothing is authenticated here, so put nothing private on it.
#
# Needs an A record like any other hostname. Point the apex and www at this box
# before `make up` — Let's Encrypt validates over HTTP on the first request,
# and a name that does not resolve yet fails that check.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 老内 — a play on 老外. The brand moved here when the app took its own name;
# liuxuesheng.io is the social product now, and setting both to that hostname is
# two Caddy site blocks on one address, which is a startup failure that takes
# every site on this box down with it.
TOMSCODING_BRAND_DOMAIN=laonei.co
# The www redirects to the apex. Give it an A record too, or comment it out.
TOMSCODING_BRAND_WWW=www.laonei.co

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXCHANGE ON THE OPEN WEB (site/index.html)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The page for somebody who has never heard of this — an investor, a
# journalist, a stranger. NOT the app's front door: that is the board's own
# hostname below, where a person with a link lands and where the waiting list
# lives. Two audiences, two pages.
#
# Caddy serves it from a file. No container, nothing to add to
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. Point the apex and www at this box BEFORE `make up` —
# Let's Encrypt validates over HTTP on the first request, and a name that does
# not resolve yet fails that check.
#
# .app is on the HSTS preload list: browsers refuse plain HTTP to it before a
# request is made. Nothing to configure for that, but it does mean a
# certificate that never got issued shows as a hard failure with no way to
# click through — so get the A record in first.
#
# Leave both unset and the site block binds an internal .localhost name and
# does nothing. Never set either to a hostname another block already claims:
# two site blocks on one address is a startup failure that takes down every
# site on this box.
TOMSCODING_SITE_DOMAIN=thexchange.app
TOMSCODING_SITE_WWW=www.thexchange.app
TOMSCODING_BRAND_NAME=Liuxuesheng
TOMSCODING_BRAND_HANZI=留学生
# The brand copy, all of it. These three lines are the page — there is no other
# text on it — so they are configuration rather than markup, and changing the
# words never means editing HTML.
#
# Every line on this page comes from docs/PITCH.md in the study-pal repo, which
# is explicit about why: "take the claims from here rather than inventing new
# ones — every number carries its source and its caveat". Nothing on the
# homepage should be a claim that is not in that file.
TOMSCODING_BRAND_TAGLINE="The app that speaks for you in China."
TOMSCODING_BRAND_BLURB="No app store, no account, no VPN — it loads inside the mainland."
# Where the live app actually is. The homepage links to it and shows whether
# this visitor's connection can reach it, which is worth knowing on a page
# aimed at people inside the firewall.
TOMSCODING_BRAND_APP_DOMAIN=liuxuesheng.help

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What public records say about who runs all this. `make privacy`.
#
# Domains are picked up from the *_DOMAIN settings above; name any others here
# — ones served from a different box still belong in the same answer.
#
# Repositories are checked without a token, on purpose: that is what a stranger
# sees. A public repo matters more than any WHOIS setting, because its commits
# carry a real name and email and its README names the domains — a searchable
# link between the two that needs no legal process to follow, and a one-way
# door, since going private later does not unpublish what was already fetched.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOMSCODING_PRIVACY_DOMAINS="fernsocial.io, sagejourney.io"
TOMSCODING_PRIVACY_REPOS="tommyshanahan-max/sage, tommyshanahan-max/study-pal, tommyshanahan-max/journey, tommyshanahan-max/sage-aesthetic, tommyshanahan-max/THE-MESS"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The counter.
#
# How many people used it today, how many of them had never been before, and
# which pages and functions they used. One small container, its own hostname,
# its own password.
#
# Off by default: add "analytics" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above and give the
# hostname an A record.
#
# The password is optional and currently not set. Without one the dashboard is
# open to anyone with the address — and every hostname is public the moment its
# certificate issues, so that means anyone who looks. The page says so on its
# face while that is true. Add TOMSCODING_STATS_PASSWORD to .env to close it;
# nothing else changes.
#
# What it counts is a random id this site puts in each browser's own storage.
# No account, no IP address kept, no third party, nothing cross-site — so
# "people" means browsers, and the same person on a phone and a laptop is two.
# There is no honest way around that without asking people to sign in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On tomscoding.com, not on the brand domain. Only liuxuesheng.help is public
# facing; liuxuesheng.io is the brand's front door and should carry nothing but
# brand. A dashboard of real usage numbers is infrastructure, and it belongs
# with the rest of the infrastructure — which also means one fewer thing a
# passer-by on the brand domain can stumble into while it has no password.
TOMSCODING_STATS_DOMAIN=numbers.tomscoding.com
# The sign-in name, used only once a password exists in .env. On its own it
# does nothing.
TOMSCODING_STATS_USER=Tom
# Which sites may report. A page on an origin that is not on this list is not
# counted, which is what keeps somebody else's copy of the snippet out of your
# numbers. Subdomains of an entry count as that entry, so www and the apex are
# one site.
#
# Every public hostname, and it is easy to be one migration behind here: an
# origin that is not listed is not counted, silently, and the panel showing a
# flat line looks exactly like a product nobody visited. laonei.co went
# uncounted from the day the brand moved to it until it was added.
TOMSCODING_STATS_SITES="laonei.co, liuxuesheng.help, liuxuesheng.io"
# How long the raw event log is kept. The per-day totals are kept forever —
# they are a few hundred bytes a day, and throwing away the record of how many
# people came is not a storage decision anyone makes on purpose.
TOMSCODING_STATS_RETAIN_DAYS=400
TOMSCODING_STATS_MEMORY=256m
TOMSCODING_STATS_CPU_SHARES=128

# Today's numbers, shown in Sage's own masthead.
#
# Sage cannot read the counter from the browser: it is a different hostname
# with its own sign-in, and that cookie is host-only. So the agent fetches it
# server-side over the Docker network — hence a container name below, not a
# public address; the request never leaves the box — and identifies itself
# with a shared token the browser never sees.
#
# Generate the token with `make password`. Without it the readout simply is
# not drawn, and the counter is unaffected.
TOMSCODING_AGENT_NUMBERS_URL=http://analytics:3000

# The app's own count, shown beside this counter's rather than added to it.
# Study Pal keeps its own tally on its own box — a page in mainland China
# cannot rely on reaching a third-party host, which is why there are two
# counters and not one. This is the public endpoint that reports it.
# The ?app= is not optional here, even though the endpoint now defaults
# correctly from the domain. One container serves Study Pal and Fern, and a
# request without it once returned Fern's number from Study Pal's domain with
# nothing on the wire to say so — a wrong figure that looks exactly like a
# right one. Naming the product costs nine characters and removes our
# dependence on somebody else's default staying what it is today.
TOMSCODING_APP_COUNT_URL=https://liuxuesheng.help/api/count?app=studypal

# And the app's usage endpoint, which is the same app answering a much better
# question: which features were actually called, on which days, by how many
# separate people, and how many of everyone who ever opened it came back.
#
# The count endpoint is public; this one is gated by the admin secret, so the
# dashboard reads it server-side and the key never reaches a browser. It reuses
# TOMSCODING_STUDYPAL_KEY — the same key the story desk already holds, because
# it is the same app and a second key would be a second thing to rotate.
#
# The compose file carries this same value as its default, so a box whose
# .env was copied from an older preset still gets it — an .env does not grow
# a line when this file does. It is here to be seen and overridden, not
# because anything breaks without it. The key is the actual lock: unset that
# and the feature bars, screen views, retention figures and arrival curve
# report a switch that is off, rather than a product nobody uses.
TOMSCODING_APP_USAGE_URL=https://liuxuesheng.help/api/usage?app=studypal
# TOMSCODING_STATS_INTERNAL_TOKEN=   (goes in .env, beside the passwords)

# Voice. Add ELEVENLABS_API_KEY to .env alongside the passwords and Sage gets a
# microphone and a voice; leave it out and she is text-only, with nothing else
# changed. The request to the speech provider is made by this server, from
# Tokyo — the browser never contacts it, same as with Anthropic.
#
# The voice id is optional; unset gives a sensible default. Pick one from your
# ElevenLabs voice library and paste its id here.
# TOMSCODING_AGENT_VOICE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A third seat: a link for somebody deciding whether to work with you
#
# The same container as Brendan's, on its own network with its own volumes and
# its own snapshot — neither seat can see the other's anything. Two things are
# different, and both follow from the same fact: this one is a LINK, and a link
# travels. Forwarded to a colleague, to a competitor, into a group chat.
#
#   TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_ROLE=prospect
#     Sage makes the case for the product, answers in the language it is
#     written to, and refuses the commercial questions — equity, revenue, who
#     else is involved, how it is hosted, how many users. Those are yours to
#     answer, and it says so rather than guessing.
#
#   TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_INCLUDE, not _EXCLUDE
#     An allow list. EXCLUDE is right for a partner: you have read the
#     repository and you know which few files are yours alone. It is wrong for
#     a stranger, because it fails open — every file added to that repository
#     next month arrives in the snapshot unless somebody remembers to name it.
#     INCLUDE fails closed. Name the pitch and the README; everything else,
#     including whatever is written tomorrow, is absent by default.
#
# Needs its own A record (partner2.tomscoding.com -> the box) before `make up`,
# its own password in .env, and `make partner-sync-2` before it will start.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_DOMAIN=partner2.tomscoding.com
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_ROLE=prospect
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_USER=
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_PROJECT=Liuxuesheng
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_REPOS="https://github.com/tommyshanahan-max/study-pal.git#main"
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_INCLUDE="README.md, docs/PITCH.md"
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_DOCS="study-pal/docs/PITCH.md=Why this exists|study-pal/README.md=What it does"
# TOMSCODING_PARTNER2_APP_URL="https://liuxuesheng.help"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Board
#
# A noticeboard for foreigners in China, standing on its own: the social side
# of Study Pal with nothing else attached — no lessons, no account, no app to
# install. Somebody posts a photograph and a line of text; everyone else can
# read it, reply, and say they have been there.
#
# It starts empty and copies nothing. The posts on the app belong to the people
# who wrote them, so this begins with a blank board and whichever accounts you
# operate.
#
# Off by default: add "board" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above and set the key below.
#
# Give it a hostname below and it is public. That is a certificate, a
# moderation duty and a thing to defend — report and block are not built, so
# think before filling this in.
#
# Every post arrives HELD. There is no model here that can judge one, so a
# person releases each by hand from the panel. That is the whole moderation
# story today — report and block are not built, and a public address should
# wait for them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Board's public address. Needs an A record pointing at this box before
# `make up`, or the certificate cannot issue and Caddy retries in a loop.
#
# THE ORDER MATTERS if this hostname is currently serving something else. Two
# Caddy site blocks on one address is a startup failure, and Caddy failing to
# start takes every other site here down with it — the seats, the agent, the
# app's counter. So: move the current occupant to its own hostname, deploy,
# check it came up, and only then set this. `make up` refuses the collision
# rather than letting you find out on the box.
#
# 留学生 — the name is the audience. Liuxuesheng.io was the brand's front door
# while the brand and the app were the same thing; the app has its own name now
# and this is the product that word actually describes.
# Somebody a reader can write to who is not this software. Shown in the feed's
# footer; unset, the page says nothing rather than printing an address that
# bounces.
#
# Worth setting before anybody outside is invited: it is what a person uses
# when they cannot use the report button, or when the thing they are reporting
# is the board itself. Apple requires it of any app carrying other people's
# words, and the reason it requires it is the reason to have it.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_CONTACT=

# MOVING THE BOARD TO ITS OWN NAME.
#
# liuxuesheng.io means "overseas student", which is what this was before it
# was about people doing business in China — and it is the name on every deck,
# every invite link and an Instagram bio. thexchange.app is the name everything
# else already uses.
#
# THREE LINES CHANGE TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL:
#
#   TOMSCODING_BOARD_DOMAIN=thexchange.app      the app moves
#   TOMSCODING_BOARD_OLD_DOMAIN=liuxuesheng.io  the old name redirects to it
#   TOMSCODING_SITE_DOMAIN=                     MUST be emptied
#
# That last one is not optional. The static site answers on thexchange.app
# today, and two Caddy blocks on one address is a startup failure that takes
# down every other site on this box. Emptied, it falls back to an internal
# .localhost and stops, which is what we want — the board serves its own
# landing page at / and does the same job.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_DOMAIN=liuxuesheng.io
TOMSCODING_BOARD_OLD_DOMAIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DOOR
#
# Empty means there is no door. The board serves the feed, the directory and
# every API to anybody with the address, and the "invite only" line at the top
# of the page is then a claim rather than a fact — it is a string in the page,
# not the gate.
#
#   read   what people mean by invite only. Everything behind a code, except
#          /about, /rules, /level and the four /r/ doors, which are the
#          deliberate holes a stranger arrives through.
#   post   anybody may read, only members may write.
#
# Changing this needs a deploy. It is the one setting here that decides whether
# the board is private at all, so check it after any change to this file.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_INVITE=read

# Private messages and groups. "off" turns every message and group route into a
# 404 without a deploy — the switch to reach for if that surface goes wrong.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_NOTES=on
# Introductions one browser may send in a day. Messages inside an open thread
# between two people who matched do not count against it.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_NOTES_A_DAY=5

# WHAT IT TAKES TO BRING SOMEBODY IN — see standing() in board/server.js.
# Days here, posts this week, the length of that week, and how many guests one
# member may have in at a time. They are here so that "this is too tight" is a
# line in this file and a restart rather than a commit.
#
# A board with no history in it wants 0 and 1: every profile on it was made
# this week, so a three-day rule refuses every member at once, including the
# people who have already brought somebody in. Tighten them once people have a
# week behind them.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_BRING_DAYS=1
TOMSCODING_BOARD_BRING_SAID=2
TOMSCODING_BOARD_BRING_WEEK=7
TOMSCODING_BOARD_GUEST_ROOM=3

# Handles that skip the standing rule. Whoever runs the board is not a member
# in the sense that rule means, and a morning when they cannot hand somebody a
# code is a morning the board stops growing. Comma separated, matched without
# case, and it is the handle on their page rather than anything about a device
# — the operator changes phones.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_STAFF=Tom
# WHO MAY BRING IN MORE THAN ONE PERSON A DAY.  "handle:number", comma
# separated.  Everybody else has the ordinary ceiling, which is a LIFETIME
# one: three guests on the board and no more codes, ever.  That is right for
# an ordinary member and wrong for the two or three people who are how a board
# this size actually fills.
#
# It is an allowance, NOT an exemption.  Every other test still applies to
# them, including the one saying the people they already brought have to have
# turned up and said something.  Somebody who should be exempt from all of it
# goes in BOARD_STAFF above instead, and think twice — "as many as you like"
# is how a private room stops being one.
#
# Names are the handles on their profiles, matched without case.  A name that
# is not on the board does nothing.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_CODES=Keith:3
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PERSON ON THE DOOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A box on the public page where somebody who has been sent a link can ask
# what this is, before deciding whether to put their name down. Answered by a
# model from a fixed written brief — see board/lib/hostess.js, which is the
# whole of what it may say.
#
# OFF unless this is exactly "on", and off is the default: a model on a public
# page is a bill anybody can run up and a voice speaking for you, and neither
# should arrive because a container was rebuilt.  It also needs
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set; without one it stays off however this reads.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. Three limits, none of which needs a database: a burst per
# browser that refills, a hard ceiling for the whole board per day, and 300
# characters per question. The default ceiling is 400 questions a day. At
# Claude Opus 5 rates with the brief cached, that is single-digit dollars a
# day at the ceiling and a few cents on an ordinary one.
#
# WHAT IT MAY NOT SAY. It is told never to claim the board is encrypted — it
# is access-controlled over HTTPS, which is a private room and not a locked
# box — never to name a member, and to say "I do not know" rather than guess.
# Those rules are in the brief; read it before turning this on.
TOMSCODING_BOARD_HOSTESS=
TOMSCODING_BOARD_HOSTESS_BURST=8
TOMSCODING_BOARD_HOSTESS_DAY=400
TOMSCODING_BOARD_WWW=www.liuxuesheng.io

# The lock on everything the admin panel does: read the queue, release, delete.
# Without it those routes refuse every request, so nothing posted could ever
# be released. `make password` generates one. This is not the same key as
# TOMSCODING_STUDYPAL_KEY and should not be set to it — that one belongs to
# somebody else's service, and one key for two products is one rotation that
# breaks both.
# TOMSCODING_BOARD_KEY=

# Salts the hash that stands in for "who posted this" — there is no account
# here, so a post is tied to a random id the browser keeps and this server
# never stores in the clear. Unsalted, that hash is a lookup away from the id
# it came from, which would make "nothing is kept about you" a claim rather
# than a fact. Generate it the same way and then leave it alone: changing it
# orphans every post from its author, so nobody can delete their own again.
# TOMSCODING_BOARD_SALT=

# Where a held post announces itself, so it reaches the panel's queue without
# anyone refreshing. The same webhook Study Pal already posts to, and the same
# secret — this is the panel's own inbox, not a third party's.
# TOMSCODING_BOARD_HOOK_URL=http://agent:3000/api/studypal-hook

# To govern the Board from the admin panel you already have, point the panel at
# it instead of the app: TOMSCODING_PARTNER_APP_URL=http://board:8080, and
# TOMSCODING_STUDYPAL_KEY set to the board key above. The panel itself needs no
# change — the Board answers the same API, to the letter, deliberately.
#
# One thing does not survive that swap. TOMSCODING_PARTNER_APP_URL is read
# twice: once by the server, as the address it calls, and once by the browser,
# as the page it puts in the phone frame. http://board:8080 resolves inside the
# box and nowhere else, so the server half works and the phone frame comes up
# blank. Everything else on that panel — the queue, the feed, releasing,
# deleting — is server-side and works. The frame gets its picture back when the
# Board has a public address, which is the point at which report and block need
# to exist anyway.
